.TH AURORA-SPEAKER-STACK 8 "2026-11-28" "Aurora Silicon" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
aurora-speaker-stack \- select the Apple Silicon speaker protection stack
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aurora-speaker-stack status
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B aurora-speaker-stack select sgv2
[\fB\-\-no\-switch\fR]
.br
.B aurora-speaker-stack select speakersafetyd
[\fB\-\-no\-install\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-switch\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B speakerguardd v2
(\fBsgv2\fR) is the default speaker protection and voicing stack on all Aurora
Silicon installations. New installs and upgraded existing installs migrate to
.B sgv2
automatically, removing the legacy Asahi speaker DSP graphs and retiring
\fBspeakerguardd\fR 0.1 and legacy Aurora \fBspeakersafetyd\fR builds from the
default package set.
.PP
Users who explicitly prefer to run \fBspeakersafetyd\fR instead of \fBsgv2\fR
can opt out with a single command:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo aurora-speaker-stack select speakersafetyd
.fi
.RE
.PP
This verifies kernel support and installs \fBspeakersafetyd\fR first (or writes
the declarative \fBaurora.speakers.stack\fR drop-in on NixOS), then masks the
\fBspeakerguardd-v2\fR and \fBaurora-speaker-upgrade\fR systemd units, switches
the kernel interlock, records the choice in
.IR /etc/aurora/speaker-stack.conf ,
installs a root \fBExecStartPre\fR drop-in
(\fI/etc/systemd/system/speakersafetyd.service.d/50-aurora-speaker-stack.conf\fR)
that arms the per-bind \fBSpeaker Protection Interface\fR control on every boot,
and starts \fBspeakersafetyd.service\fR. If package installation, interlock
hand-over, or service startup fails, the previous \fBsgv2\fR configuration is
preserved or restored and the command exits non-zero.
.PP
Note: \fBaurora-audio\fR no longer installs the Asahi speaker DSP graphs,
speaker FIRs, or the WirePlumber raw speaker volume lock. Opting into stock
\fBspeakersafetyd\fR runs \fBspeakersafetyd\fR without those Asahi speaker
profiles unless you supply them separately.
.PP
Future package upgrades read
.I /etc/aurora/speaker-stack.conf
and preserve an explicit \fBspeakersafetyd\fR opt-out instead of migrating the
machine back to \fBsgv2\fR.
.PP
To return to the default \fBspeakerguardd v2\fR stack at any time:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo aurora-speaker-stack select sgv2
.fi
.RE
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status [\-\-json]
Display the configured stack (default or explicit override), unit states, and
detected ALSA speaker interlock state. Does not require root privileges.
.TP
.B select sgv2 [\-\-no\-switch]
Select \fBspeakerguardd v2\fR, remove the \fBspeakersafetyd\fR opt-in drop-in,
mask \fBspeakersafetyd.service\fR, unmask and enable the \fBspeakerguardd-v2\fR
and \fBaurora-speaker-upgrade\fR units, and run \fBaurora-speaker-upgrade\fR
unless \fB\-\-no\-switch\fR is passed.
.TP
.B select speakersafetyd [\-\-no\-install] [\-\-no\-switch]
Verify kernel support, install \fBspeakersafetyd\fR (unless
\fB\-\-no\-install\fR), mask \fBspeakerguardd-v2\fR and
\fBaurora-speaker-upgrade\fR, hand over the kernel interlock, record an
explicit opt-out in \fI/etc/aurora/speaker-stack.conf\fR, install the root
\fBExecStartPre\fR interlock drop-in, and start \fBspeakersafetyd.service\fR.
.TP
.B interlock\-apply
Internal \fBExecStartPre\fR helper for \fBspeakersafetyd.service\fR. Fails
closed unless \fI/etc/aurora/speaker-stack.conf\fR explicitly selects
\fBspeakersafetyd\fR; when selected, writes \fBSpeaker Volume Unlock\fR to
\fBSpeaker Protection Interface\fR as root.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/aurora/speaker-stack.conf
Persistent user selection (\fBstack=sgv2\fR or \fBstack=speakersafetyd\fR).
Absent by default, which means \fBsgv2\fR. On NixOS this file is managed
declaratively by \fBaurora.speakers.stack\fR.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/speakersafetyd.service.d/50-aurora-speaker-stack.conf
Systemd drop-in installed on explicit \fBspeakersafetyd\fR opt-out (Fedora and
Arch Linux ARM) so the per-bind kernel interlock is armed before
\fBspeakersafetyd\fR starts on each boot.
.TP
.I /etc/nixos/aurora-speaker-stack.nix
Generated NixOS module override written on NixOS systems.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aurora-speaker-upgrade (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
